26
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:02,656
    - "Ark 1" is the first of   
    a long line of spacecraft   

27
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:04,291
     fleeing a failing Earth    

28
00:00:04,358 --> 00:00:06,761
    in search of a new planet   
  to call home.                 

29
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:08,763
   The crew was tested          
  along the way.                

30
00:00:08,829 --> 00:00:10,564
 - Lane!                        

31
00:00:10,631 --> 00:00:12,433
    - These implants,           
  they give her                 

32
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,502
   some kind of                 
   superhuman strength?         

33
00:00:14,568 --> 00:00:16,303
   - I thought you were dead.   
  - What are you talking about? 

34
00:00:16,370 --> 00:00:18,906
    - What, you really didn't   
  know you were a clone?        

35
00:00:18,973 --> 00:00:20,441
   - We were able to make peace 

36
00:00:20,508 --> 00:00:22,109
  with the rebel                
   Eastern Federation.          

37
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:23,811
   - This is Captain Zed Avega. 

38
00:00:23,878 --> 00:00:25,212
 - Come with us.                

39
00:00:25,279 --> 00:00:27,915
   - Together,                  
    we made it to Trappist-1D.  

40
00:00:27,982 --> 00:00:29,316
    - There's obviously water   

41
00:00:29,383 --> 00:00:30,985
    and the atmosphere          
   is similar to Earth.         

42
00:00:31,052 --> 00:00:33,421
    - But the team at Homebase  
  has begun to discover         

43
00:00:33,487 --> 00:00:36,791
    that our new home           
    holds secrets of its own.   

44
00:00:36,857 --> 00:00:39,460
    Other ships are still out   
    there looking for a home.   

45
00:00:39,527 --> 00:00:41,595
    "Ark 1" won't stop          
    searching for them          

46
00:00:41,662 --> 00:00:43,330
    until they bring them back  
   to Homebase.                 

47
00:00:43,397 --> 00:00:45,199
  - Do not come to Ross!        

48
00:00:45,266 --> 00:00:48,002
    Turn around before          
  it's too late!                

49
00:00:57,078 --> 00:00:59,780
   - So set a course for Ross?  

50
00:00:59,847 --> 00:01:01,882
 - Their message sounded        
like they're in trouble.        

51
00:01:01,949 --> 00:01:04,385
- Well, exactly. It said        
  do not come here, didn't it?  

52
00:01:04,452 --> 00:01:06,854
  - Ian, our mission is         
  to find people who need help  

53
00:01:06,921 --> 00:01:08,222
  and take them to Homebase 1.  

54
00:01:08,289 --> 00:01:09,690
 - I say we go.                 

55
00:01:09,757 --> 00:01:11,092
   [suspenseful music]          

56
00:01:11,158 --> 00:01:12,426
 - Alicia, are we close         
 enough yet to get more         

57
00:01:12,493 --> 00:01:14,528
  information on the situation? 

58
00:01:14,595 --> 00:01:18,232
   ♪ ♪                          

59
00:01:18,299 --> 00:01:20,067
 - Still too far                
 for planetary readings,        

60
00:01:20,134 --> 00:01:21,135
 but we've moved                
into range to get               

61
00:01:21,202 --> 00:01:22,636
 some readings on the Ross sun. 

62
00:01:22,703 --> 00:01:25,139
 It's throwing off some         
   really severe solar storms.  

63
00:01:25,206 --> 00:01:26,507
   - Meaning?                   

64
00:01:26,574 --> 00:01:27,975
   - "Ark 4" doesn't have the   
long-range sensors we do.       

65
00:01:28,042 --> 00:01:29,777
   It wouldn't have been able   
   to detect the solar storms   

66
00:01:29,844 --> 00:01:30,945
 until it was too late.         

67
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:32,413
  - Too late for what?          

68
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:34,115
  - Solar storms at that level  
 would have knocked out         

69
00:01:34,181 --> 00:01:36,117
all life support                
 and electrical systems         

70
00:01:36,183 --> 00:01:38,219
on "Ark 4" before               
 it dropped out of FTL.         

71
00:01:38,285 --> 00:01:39,753
 - So they're all dead?         

72
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:41,055
   - Not necessarily.           

73
00:01:41,122 --> 00:01:42,022
   It's possible they           
 made it to the surface         

74
00:01:42,089 --> 00:01:43,257
before the storm started.       

75
00:01:43,324 --> 00:01:44,625
 - But wouldn't the solar storm 
  still be bad                  

76
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:45,926
   down on the planet?          

77
00:01:45,993 --> 00:01:48,229
 - The atmosphere would protect 
the crew from radiation.        

78
00:01:48,295 --> 00:01:49,730
   - Then there might           
  be survivors.                 

79
00:01:49,797 --> 00:01:51,265
- Wait. If we go,               
 won't our life support systems 

80
00:01:51,332 --> 00:01:52,766
 just be fried like theirs was? 

81
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:54,602
   - Actually,                  
   I've been working on a way   

82
00:01:54,668 --> 00:01:56,070
  to rig the artificial         
gravity generator               

83
00:01:56,137 --> 00:01:57,338
 to project some                
of its gravitons                

84
00:01:57,404 --> 00:01:58,873
 onto the exterior of the ship. 

85
00:01:58,939 --> 00:02:00,241
 - Which would do what?         

86
00:02:00,307 --> 00:02:02,309
   - It would act as a shield   
against the solar storms.       

87
00:02:02,376 --> 00:02:03,911
- There's a "but" coming.       

88
00:02:03,978 --> 00:02:05,479
 I can always sense when        
 there's a "but" on the way out 

89
00:02:05,546 --> 00:02:07,081
  of that little mouth of hers. 

90
00:02:07,148 --> 00:02:08,616
 - No "but." It'll work.        

91
00:02:08,682 --> 00:02:09,650
- Great.                        

92
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:10,718
   - For three hours.           

93
00:02:10,784 --> 00:02:11,886
   - And there it is.           

94
00:02:11,952 --> 00:02:13,988
 - That wasn't a "but."         

95
00:02:14,054 --> 00:02:15,356
  - All right,                  
  then we'll move fast          

96
00:02:15,422 --> 00:02:17,825
and use those three hours       
to search for survivors.        

97
00:02:17,892 --> 00:02:18,926
   How long until we're there?  

98
00:02:18,993 --> 00:02:20,461
  - Two hours.                  

99
00:02:20,528 --> 00:02:22,129
   - Call me when we're close.  

100
00:02:22,196 --> 00:02:25,633
 [peaceful music]               

101
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:31,739
   ♪ ♪                          

102
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,408
  - Mr. Trust!                  

103
00:02:34,475 --> 00:02:36,644
You're not gonna believe        
  this slab we dug up!          

104
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:39,980
Oh, man.                        

105
00:02:40,047 --> 00:02:43,017
   Angus to Mr. Trust.          

106
00:02:43,083 --> 00:02:44,718
   Mr. Trust!                   

107
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:46,287
 - The man never                
 answers his WD.                

108
00:02:46,353 --> 00:02:47,555
  So annoying.                  

109
00:02:47,621 --> 00:02:49,089
  - All right.                  

110
00:02:49,156 --> 00:02:51,992
   Uh-- sorry, can you just--   

111
00:02:52,059 --> 00:02:53,928
 - Yeah.                        
  - Thank you so much.          

112
00:02:53,994 --> 00:02:56,864
 [keypad beeping]               

113
00:02:56,931 --> 00:02:58,766
   OK.                          

114
00:02:58,832 --> 00:03:00,868
   Thank you so much.           

115
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:05,072
   Oh, that's so cool.          

116
00:03:05,139 --> 00:03:06,240
 [keypad beeping]               

117
00:03:06,307 --> 00:03:07,508
   OK, let's just go find him.  

118
00:03:07,575 --> 00:03:08,542
  - OK.                         

119
00:03:11,879 --> 00:03:15,583
  [tense music]                 

120
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:19,553
  - All right.                  

121
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:22,056
   - Hi, James.                 
  You're right on time.         

122
00:03:22,122 --> 00:03:23,924
 - Eva.                         

123
00:03:23,991 --> 00:03:26,160
Wow, you uh-- [chuckles]        

124
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:29,496
 You look absolutely ravishing. 

125
00:03:29,563 --> 00:03:32,299
 - Well, I wanted to look       
   my best for our celebration. 

126
00:03:32,366 --> 00:03:34,168
   - Oh, yeah?                  

127
00:03:34,235 --> 00:03:35,302
  What we celebrating?          

128
00:03:35,369 --> 00:03:37,037
   - Our one-year anniversary.  

129
00:03:37,104 --> 00:03:40,708
   You kissed me for the first  
 time exactly a year ago.       

130
00:03:40,774 --> 00:03:42,376
 - Huh.                         

131
00:03:42,443 --> 00:03:43,844
 Is that right?                 
Exactly?                        

132
00:03:43,911 --> 00:03:46,380
   - [chuckles]                 
  OK, about a year ago.         

133
00:03:46,447 --> 00:03:47,648
 Just go with it.               

134
00:03:47,715 --> 00:03:49,350
  - All right,                  
  all right, all right.         

135
00:03:49,416 --> 00:03:54,521
    - So look what I found at   
  the Homebase exchange office. 

136
00:03:57,324 --> 00:04:00,527
  A woman from "Ark 11"         
 was trading it.                

137
00:04:00,594 --> 00:04:02,196
  - Oh!                         

138
00:04:02,263 --> 00:04:03,931
   Oh!                          

139
00:04:03,998 --> 00:04:05,766
   Wait, do-- do you know how   
  many light years away from me 

140
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:06,900
   you are right now?           

141
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:08,168
 That is just so cruel.         

142
00:04:08,235 --> 00:04:10,271
  - Just pretend                
    I'm right there with you.   

143
00:04:10,337 --> 00:04:12,539
 - Oh, baby, I want to.         

144
00:04:12,606 --> 00:04:14,341
   I really, like, really do.   

145
00:04:14,408 --> 00:04:16,110
But, um--[clicks tongue]        

146
00:04:16,176 --> 00:04:20,014
   Listen, could we maybe save  
this for tomorrow's call?       

147
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:21,282
 - What?                        

148
00:04:21,348 --> 00:04:22,383
  - Because we're about         
to land at Ross,                

149
00:04:22,449 --> 00:04:24,618
   and I don't really           
 have any time.                 

150
00:04:24,685 --> 00:04:26,053
 I just wanted to see your face 

151
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,855
before we go down               
 to the planet.                 

152
00:04:27,921 --> 00:04:30,958
    - You're going down there?  
   Is it safe?                  

153
00:04:31,025 --> 00:04:33,193
  - Is it safe?                 
 Y-yes.                         

154
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:35,529
Yes, of course it's safe.       

155
00:04:35,596 --> 00:04:38,532
  Listen, I'll tell you         
   all about it tomorrow, OK?   

156
00:04:38,599 --> 00:04:41,201
    - All personnel, we arrive  
 at Ross in ten minutes.        

157
00:04:41,268 --> 00:04:45,205
- [sighs]                       
 All right, I gotta go.         

158
00:04:45,272 --> 00:04:47,274
   I love you.                  
    - I love you, too.          

159
00:04:47,341 --> 00:04:48,742
  - [chuckles]                  

160
00:04:53,647 --> 00:04:55,316
- All these times               
  we've been in an FTL bubble,  

161
00:04:55,382 --> 00:04:57,718
I never realized                
  how beautiful it is.          

162
00:04:57,785 --> 00:05:00,254
 - Yeah.                        

163
00:05:00,321 --> 00:05:03,590
 It is kind of stunning,        
isn't it, Curly?                

164
00:05:03,657 --> 00:05:06,193
- Curly?                        

165
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:07,328
- Yeah, that was                
   my nickname for you          

166
00:05:07,394 --> 00:05:08,829
 when we were in the coma dream 
together.                       

167
00:05:08,896 --> 00:05:11,732
  You remember.                 
 - No, I don't.                 

168
00:05:11,799 --> 00:05:14,301
  - Well, sure you do.          
  You loved it.                 

169
00:05:14,368 --> 00:05:16,403
 - I really doubt that.         

170
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:17,971
- Yeah, I started               
 calling you Curly when         

171
00:05:18,038 --> 00:05:19,373
   we were about 20 years in.   

172
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:20,808
We were deep into               
  our romance by then.          

173
00:05:20,874 --> 00:05:23,610
 - Hmm.                         
Did I call you something?       

174
00:05:23,677 --> 00:05:25,746
 - Yeah.                        

175
00:05:25,813 --> 00:05:27,247
  Ian.                          

176
00:05:27,314 --> 00:05:30,017
  - [chuckles]                  

177
00:05:32,219 --> 00:05:34,121
   Oh.                          

178
00:05:34,188 --> 00:05:37,791
  Ian, we've been through this. 

179
00:05:37,858 --> 00:05:40,661
- [sighs]                       

180
00:05:40,728 --> 00:05:41,995
You still hung up               
  on the whole                  

181
00:05:42,062 --> 00:05:43,197
   it was just a dream thing.   

182
00:05:43,263 --> 00:05:45,833
 - It was just a dream.         

183
00:05:45,899 --> 00:05:47,601
- Maybe.                        

184
00:05:47,668 --> 00:05:51,038
   But a wonderful, beautiful,  
 romantic dream.                

185
00:05:51,105 --> 00:05:52,973
  - [chuckles]                  

186
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,043
OK, yes, it was.                

187
00:05:56,110 --> 00:05:59,012
But, Ian, it wasn't real.       

188
00:05:59,079 --> 00:06:02,049
   - Feelings I was left with   
 felt pretty real to me.        

189
00:06:02,116 --> 00:06:06,987
   And I think they're          
   real for you, too.           

190
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:09,390
 You just won't admit it        
for some reason.                

191
00:06:09,456 --> 00:06:11,892
  - I'm sorry.                  
   OK?                          

192
00:06:11,959 --> 00:06:14,895
   I'm dealing with a delicate  
balance on this ship now,       

193
00:06:14,962 --> 00:06:17,598
  commanding former GSA         
and EF personnel                

194
00:06:17,664 --> 00:06:19,266
   who just weeks ago           
  were enemies.                 

195
00:06:19,333 --> 00:06:21,402
   And somehow, God knows how,  

196
00:06:21,468 --> 00:06:24,004
  I've managed                  
  to gain their trust, and I--  

197
00:06:24,071 --> 00:06:26,673
  I don't want to squander it.  

198
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:28,809
 - And being seen to be         
   in a romantic relationship   

199
00:06:28,876 --> 00:06:32,413
   with a member of your crew   
ain't very professional.        

200
00:06:32,479 --> 00:06:33,881
I get it.                       

201
00:06:33,947 --> 00:06:39,219
   ♪ ♪                          

202
00:06:39,286 --> 00:06:41,789
- We're at Ross.                
Let's get to the bridge.        

203
00:06:41,855 --> 00:06:45,292
   ♪ ♪                          

204
00:06:45,359 --> 00:06:46,593
 Report.                        

205
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:47,961
   - The solar shields          
  are working.                  

206
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:49,496
   - Good job, Alicia.          

207
00:06:49,563 --> 00:06:50,898
 - The scans are showing        
no signs of life                

208
00:06:50,964 --> 00:06:52,900
 on the parts of "Ark 4"        
 still in orbit.                

209
00:06:52,966 --> 00:06:55,369
   It's not responding          
   to remote commands.          

210
00:06:55,436 --> 00:06:56,737
  Electrics are fried.          

211
00:06:56,804 --> 00:06:58,372
  - That includes life support, 
   I'm afraid.                  

212
00:06:58,439 --> 00:07:00,474
 - So everyone is dead?         

213
00:07:00,541 --> 00:07:02,242
- On the section                
  still in orbit, yes.          

214
00:07:02,309 --> 00:07:03,944
 But they managed to get        
two of their aft sections       

215
00:07:04,011 --> 00:07:05,179
  and a center section          
 to the surface.                

216
00:07:05,245 --> 00:07:07,347
   I'm guessing before          
  the solar storms hit.         

217
00:07:07,414 --> 00:07:08,515
  - Any signs of life?          
 - Yes.                         

218
00:07:08,582 --> 00:07:09,650
  There are several life signs. 

219
00:07:09,716 --> 00:07:10,984
   We may have gotten           
  here in time.                 

220
00:07:11,051 --> 00:07:13,120
  - All right.                  
   Let's go find them.          

221
00:07:13,187 --> 00:07:15,989
  Kimi, Brice, you're with me.  

222
00:07:16,056 --> 00:07:17,558
  Dr. Morozov,                  
   meet us in the shuttle dock  

223
00:07:17,624 --> 00:07:18,659
  for a rescue mission.         

224
00:07:18,725 --> 00:07:22,062
 Full EVA suits                 
  and security batons.          

225
00:07:22,129 --> 00:07:24,565
   - The shields will hold for  
 another 2 hours and 52 minutes,

226
00:07:24,631 --> 00:07:27,067
so hurry.                       
  - OK.                         

227
00:07:27,134 --> 00:07:28,302
 - I'm coming with you.         

228
00:07:28,368 --> 00:07:29,770
- No, you're not.               

229
00:07:29,837 --> 00:07:33,173
 Alicia might need your         
 help with engineering.         

230
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:34,808
- Sharon.                       

231
00:07:37,044 --> 00:07:38,378
 Be careful down there.         

232
00:07:38,445 --> 00:07:41,381
  [tense music]                 

233
00:07:41,448 --> 00:07:47,588
   ♪ ♪                          

234
00:07:48,889 --> 00:07:51,959
 - Looks kind of like Homebase. 

235
00:07:52,025 --> 00:07:53,660
- Earth-like planets were       
 the only ones selected         

236
00:07:53,727 --> 00:07:55,562
   for colonies, Dr. Morozov.   

237
00:07:55,629 --> 00:07:57,397
  - Oh, I'm technically         
  not a doctor.                 

238
00:07:57,464 --> 00:07:59,199
   More a field medic.          

239
00:07:59,266 --> 00:08:01,268
  But people still call me Doc. 

240
00:08:01,335 --> 00:08:02,870
  - Doc it is.                  

241
00:08:02,936 --> 00:08:04,605
 I'm sorry we didn't get        
  a chance to talk more         

242
00:08:04,671 --> 00:08:06,173
before being thrown into        
   the fire together.           

243
00:08:08,775 --> 00:08:10,677
Could that damage               
  be from the landing?          

244
00:08:10,744 --> 00:08:12,179
 - Probably not.                

245
00:08:12,246 --> 00:08:14,515
  The lower structures designed 
   for touchdown look intact.   

246
00:08:14,581 --> 00:08:15,649
- Does that mean                
something caused                

247
00:08:15,716 --> 00:08:18,485
  the damage after they landed? 

248
00:08:18,552 --> 00:08:21,288
   - Could be.                  
Eyes open, folks.               

249
00:08:21,355 --> 00:08:22,890
Let's stay safe.                

250
00:08:22,956 --> 00:08:25,492
   ♪ ♪                          

251
00:08:25,559 --> 00:08:27,160
[grunts]                        

252
00:08:27,227 --> 00:08:34,167
   ♪ ♪                          

253
00:08:38,505 --> 00:08:40,941
- The air inside is safe.       

254
00:08:41,008 --> 00:08:45,879
 We should take off the helmets 
  so we don't overheat.         

255
00:08:45,946 --> 00:08:48,582
- [sighs]                       

256
00:08:48,649 --> 00:08:50,517
 - How about we take off        
  the whole damn suit?          

257
00:08:50,584 --> 00:08:51,952
  - Fine by me.                 

258
00:08:52,019 --> 00:08:55,355
I should warn you I'm not       
  wearing anything underneath.  

259
00:08:55,422 --> 00:08:58,191
 - You guys smell that?         

260
00:08:58,258 --> 00:09:01,328
   - [sniffs]                   

261
00:09:01,395 --> 00:09:03,196
   - [coughs]                   

262
00:09:03,263 --> 00:09:05,532
   - [coughs]                   

263
00:09:05,599 --> 00:09:08,101
   - [coughs]                   

264
00:09:08,168 --> 00:09:09,603
Oh, God.                        

265
00:09:09,670 --> 00:09:11,672
- Oh, the stench.               

266
00:09:11,738 --> 00:09:18,845
   ♪ ♪                          

267
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:22,649
- These are some                
   really deep wounds.          

268
00:09:22,716 --> 00:09:24,751
 - Like from the                
  building collapsing?          

269
00:09:24,818 --> 00:09:27,254
   - More like someone          
 sliced and diced them.         

270
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:30,023
  - That can't be good.         

271
00:09:30,090 --> 00:09:31,858
   - I wonder who they          
armed themselves against.       

272
00:09:31,925 --> 00:09:34,161
 - I don't know,                
 but let's do the same.         

273
00:09:34,227 --> 00:09:36,797
   ♪ ♪                          

274
00:09:36,863 --> 00:09:39,366
   - Doc, scan to see           
   if anyone is alive.          

275
00:09:39,433 --> 00:09:41,535
   ♪ ♪                          

276
00:09:41,602 --> 00:09:43,770
    [scanner beeping]           

277
00:09:43,837 --> 00:09:45,072
 [scanner chimes]               

278
00:09:45,138 --> 00:09:47,474
   ♪ ♪                          

279
00:09:47,541 --> 00:09:49,710
 Anyone?                        

280
00:09:49,776 --> 00:09:54,648
   ♪ ♪                          

281
00:09:54,715 --> 00:09:56,183
   Let's keep moving.           

282
00:09:56,249 --> 00:09:58,118
  [tense music]                 

283
00:10:00,420 --> 00:10:01,722
   [low chime]                  

284
00:10:01,788 --> 00:10:03,390
  - An X10-class flare.         

285
00:10:03,457 --> 00:10:04,558
  - Well, what does that mean?  

286
00:10:04,625 --> 00:10:05,759
   - Means the shields          
 took a big hit.                

287
00:10:05,826 --> 00:10:07,427
 They won't last as long        
  as I thought.                 

288
00:10:07,494 --> 00:10:10,330
   We have to tell the surface  
team they have less time.       

289
00:10:10,397 --> 00:10:13,133
   - No, wait. I might have an  
 idea that could buy them more. 

290
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:14,167
[laughs]                        
- You do?                       

291
00:10:14,234 --> 00:10:16,303
 - Yeah.                        
   Don't sound too surprised.   

292
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:19,806
   I know a lot about           
  graviton generators.          

293
00:10:19,873 --> 00:10:21,608
  - Oh, my God,                 
   what are you doing?          

294
00:10:21,675 --> 00:10:22,909
 - What?                        
   - Those commands would have  

295
00:10:22,976 --> 00:10:24,177
 turned off the                 
   artificial gravity.          

296
00:10:24,244 --> 00:10:26,279
  We'd all be floating          
 on the ceiling.                

297
00:10:26,346 --> 00:10:27,581
 - Wait, really?                
 - Yes.                         

298
00:10:27,648 --> 00:10:30,784
It wouldn't have                
   helped the shields at all.   

299
00:10:30,851 --> 00:10:34,621
  - Uh, I mean, I guess I must  
  be off my game a bit.         

300
00:10:34,688 --> 00:10:35,922
   I'm sorry.                   

301
00:10:35,989 --> 00:10:37,791
  - We'll have to think         
   of something else.           

302
00:10:37,858 --> 00:10:40,761
  [tense music]                 

303
00:10:40,827 --> 00:10:45,432
   ♪ ♪                          

304
00:10:45,499 --> 00:10:49,169
    [scanner beeping]           

305
00:10:49,903 --> 00:10:51,271
   - They're all dead.          

306
00:10:51,338 --> 00:10:53,674
 Similar wounds.                

307
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:55,242
- What the hell did this?       

308
00:10:55,308 --> 00:10:56,710
 [distant thud]                 

309
00:10:56,777 --> 00:10:59,746
 - Hey, did you guys hear that? 

310
00:10:59,813 --> 00:11:00,847
 - Defensive formation.         

311
00:11:00,914 --> 00:11:02,649
Kimi, take rear.                
Doc, stay in the middle.        

312
00:11:02,716 --> 00:11:08,822
   ♪ ♪                          

313
00:11:14,327 --> 00:11:15,829
 [distant thud]                 

314
00:11:15,896 --> 00:11:22,035
   ♪ ♪                          

315
00:11:44,991 --> 00:11:47,761
- I told you not to come.       

316
00:11:58,505 --> 00:11:58,538
 -                              

317
00:11:58,538 --> 00:11:58,572
 - Wh                           

318
00:11:58,572 --> 00:11:58,605
 - Why                          

319
00:11:58,605 --> 00:11:58,638
 - Why di                       

320
00:11:58,638 --> 00:11:58,672
 - Why didn                     

321
00:11:58,672 --> 00:11:58,705
 - Why didn't                   

322
00:11:58,705 --> 00:11:58,739
 - Why didn't t                 

323
00:11:58,739 --> 00:11:58,772
 - Why didn't the               

324
00:11:58,772 --> 00:11:58,805
 - Why didn't the c             

325
00:11:58,805 --> 00:11:58,839
 - Why didn't the col           

326
00:11:58,839 --> 00:11:58,872
 - Why didn't the colon         

327
00:11:58,872 --> 00:11:59,039
 - Why didn't the colony        

328
00:11:59,039 --> 00:11:59,072
 - Why didn't the colony        
 ch                             

329
00:11:59,072 --> 00:11:59,106
 - Why didn't the colony        
 chec                           

330
00:11:59,106 --> 00:11:59,139
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check                          

331
00:11:59,139 --> 00:11:59,172
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check th                       

332
00:11:59,172 --> 00:11:59,206
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the                      

333
00:11:59,206 --> 00:11:59,239
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the ai                   

334
00:11:59,239 --> 00:11:59,272
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the air                  

335
00:11:59,272 --> 00:11:59,306
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the air ea               

336
00:11:59,306 --> 00:11:59,339
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the air earl             

337
00:11:59,339 --> 00:11:59,372
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the air earlie           

338
00:11:59,372 --> 00:12:00,841
 - Why didn't the colony        
 check the air earlier?         

339
00:12:00,907 --> 00:12:03,477
  - They did, but I don't trust 
  any study done under Maddox,  

340
00:12:03,543 --> 00:12:05,912
   so I'm double-checking it.   

341
00:12:05,979 --> 00:12:08,515
  - Roger that.                 

342
00:12:08,582 --> 00:12:11,651
5 kilometers out                
from the sanitation zone.       

343
00:12:11,718 --> 00:12:17,190
  - Nitrogen, 77%, oxygen 21.8, 
  carbon dioxide, 0.02,         

344
00:12:17,257 --> 00:12:19,960
   and 4% water vapor.          

345
00:12:20,026 --> 00:12:22,095
The air could not               
be more perfect.                

346
00:12:22,162 --> 00:12:24,898
  - Almost like it was          
made just for us.               

347
00:12:24,965 --> 00:12:26,767
 - Let me know when we turn out.

348
00:12:26,833 --> 00:12:29,770
 [soft suspenseful music]       

349
00:12:29,836 --> 00:12:31,738
   ♪ ♪                          

350
00:12:31,805 --> 00:12:35,108
 - I'm sorry about your brother.
 I know he loved you very much. 

351
00:12:35,175 --> 00:12:38,612
He never stopped                
   searching for you.           

352
00:12:38,678 --> 00:12:40,313
- Thank you, sir.               

353
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:41,548
   - We're all civilians now.   

354
00:12:41,615 --> 00:12:44,151
 I'm not your commanding officer
anymore.                        

355
00:12:44,217 --> 00:12:46,620
  My given name is Zed.         

356
00:12:46,686 --> 00:12:48,088
  - OK.                         

357
00:12:48,155 --> 00:12:49,823
- 10 kilometers.                

358
00:12:49,890 --> 00:12:52,292
    [beeping]                   

359
00:12:52,359 --> 00:12:54,394
   Look, Eva--                  

360
00:12:54,461 --> 00:12:56,530
   may I call you Eva?          

361
00:12:56,596 --> 00:12:58,965
 - Yes.                         

362
00:12:59,032 --> 00:13:00,433
 - Eva, I want you to know that 

363
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:04,070
   the EF and the GSA           
  don't exist anymore.          

364
00:13:04,137 --> 00:13:06,606
All that's behind us now.       

365
00:13:06,673 --> 00:13:09,109
   - Yes, sir.                  

366
00:13:09,176 --> 00:13:10,744
   I mean Zed.                  

367
00:13:10,811 --> 00:13:12,445
  - Mm.                         

368
00:13:12,512 --> 00:13:14,781
   - Any luck finding           
   the other EF ship?           

369
00:13:14,848 --> 00:13:17,918
- "EF1"?                        
   No.                          

370
00:13:17,984 --> 00:13:21,688
   We've been trying to raise   
 them, but no response.         

371
00:13:21,755 --> 00:13:25,091
- I had a lot of friends        
on there.                       

372
00:13:25,158 --> 00:13:26,860
- My son is on that ship.       

373
00:13:26,927 --> 00:13:29,863
  [somber music]                

374
00:13:29,930 --> 00:13:32,199
   ♪ ♪                          

375
00:13:32,265 --> 00:13:34,367
  - How far are we now?         

376
00:13:34,434 --> 00:13:36,837
  - 14 kilometers from          
  the sanitation zone.          

377
00:13:36,903 --> 00:13:40,140
  - The readings out here have  
 changed from Maddox's readings.

378
00:13:40,207 --> 00:13:41,775
Air quality is decaying.        

379
00:13:41,842 --> 00:13:44,377
Oxygen is down to               
almost half what it was.        

380
00:13:44,444 --> 00:13:47,814
 Carbon dioxide                 
  and nitrogen are up.          

381
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:51,318
   And the rest is just argon   
 and--and sulfuric acid.        

382
00:13:51,384 --> 00:13:53,153
  - Why would that be?          
 - I don't know.                

383
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:56,923
  - 21k. Check it now.          

384
00:13:56,990 --> 00:13:58,859
  - It's worse.                 
 8% O2.                         

385
00:13:58,925 --> 00:14:00,427
Sulfuric acid is rising.        

386
00:14:00,493 --> 00:14:01,928
- So what does this mean?       

387
00:14:01,995 --> 00:14:03,263
 - It means we can only         

388
00:14:03,330 --> 00:14:05,732
   expand the exploration zone  
   out to 15k.                  

389
00:14:05,799 --> 00:14:07,834
 Any more, and we won't         
   be able to breathe.          

390
00:14:07,901 --> 00:14:10,103
  - We should check it          
every day or so,                

391
00:14:10,170 --> 00:14:12,906
  make sure the bad air         
   isn't expanding towards us.  

392
00:14:12,973 --> 00:14:14,908
   - It better not be.          

393
00:14:14,975 --> 00:14:19,179
Or it could mean the end        
 of our peaceful little colony. 

394
00:14:19,246 --> 00:14:21,948
   [laughter]                   
- No, seriously.                

395
00:14:22,015 --> 00:14:23,917
   No.                          
  I didn't make it up.          

396
00:14:23,984 --> 00:14:25,552
Honestly, I can't believe       

397
00:14:25,619 --> 00:14:28,455
   you've never heard           
   the term afternoon delight.  

398
00:14:28,521 --> 00:14:31,958
   - Mm, nope.                  
 - Hmm.                         

399
00:14:32,025 --> 00:14:33,360
  - But it's very descriptive.  

400
00:14:33,426 --> 00:14:36,129
- Mm-hmm.                       
   [chuckles]                   

401
00:14:36,196 --> 00:14:38,598
- Matter of fact,               

402
00:14:38,665 --> 00:14:41,134
   I'd like some more           
of that delight.                

403
00:14:41,201 --> 00:14:42,269
 - Now?                         

404
00:14:42,335 --> 00:14:43,336
 - Yeah.                        

405
00:14:43,403 --> 00:14:44,638
  - But again?                  

406
00:14:44,704 --> 00:14:46,339
I don't want you                
   to burn out on me.           

407
00:14:46,406 --> 00:14:48,441
  - Oh, never.                  
  - [chuckles]                  

408
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:52,612
 - I love you too much for that.

409
00:14:56,316 --> 00:14:58,118
- You do?                       

410
00:14:58,184 --> 00:14:59,619
 - Yeah.                        

411
00:14:59,686 --> 00:15:02,155
  [soft dramatic music]         

412
00:15:02,222 --> 00:15:05,125
- Me too.                       

413
00:15:05,191 --> 00:15:06,826
   [chuckles]                   

414
00:15:06,893 --> 00:15:09,629
   - So we should only          
   widen the exploration zone   

415
00:15:09,696 --> 00:15:13,500
   10 kilometers out,           
 but the air is safe out to 15k.

416
00:15:13,566 --> 00:15:17,337
 - Then we should start         
 exploring the exploration zone 

417
00:15:17,404 --> 00:15:18,872
 out to that limit immediately. 

418
00:15:18,939 --> 00:15:20,173
- I don't agree.                

419
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:22,375
   It's not safe to go outside  
  our established zone.         

420
00:15:22,442 --> 00:15:23,643
   - We're explorers.           

421
00:15:23,710 --> 00:15:25,278
 We can't explore our new planet

422
00:15:25,345 --> 00:15:27,447
without venturing               
outside our comfort zone.       

423
00:15:27,514 --> 00:15:30,050
- And now that my ship's        
   population have offloaded,   

424
00:15:30,116 --> 00:15:33,019
   there's far too many of us   
crowded in a tight space.       

425
00:15:33,086 --> 00:15:35,689
And hopefully, "EF1" will       
  be arriving shortly.          

426
00:15:35,755 --> 00:15:38,625
We need to expand               
 our footprint.                 

427
00:15:40,126 --> 00:15:42,696
 - [whispering]                 

428
00:15:45,031 --> 00:15:47,467
   - Two men and a woman were   
  beaten today                  

429
00:15:47,534 --> 00:15:49,069
walking down the street.        

430
00:15:49,135 --> 00:15:50,236
 - Why?                         

431
00:15:50,303 --> 00:15:54,507
 - For the crime                
   of having been EF.           

432
00:15:54,574 --> 00:15:56,309
 - I'm sorry, Mr. Avega.        

433
00:15:56,376 --> 00:15:58,311
Old animosities die hard.       

434
00:15:58,378 --> 00:15:59,779
 - Well, it has to stop.        

435
00:15:59,846 --> 00:16:01,715
I don't have time               
   to keep breaking up fights.  

436
00:16:01,781 --> 00:16:03,249
   I have too much on           
   my plate as it is.           

437
00:16:03,316 --> 00:16:04,517
  - OK, then we'll find         
  someone else                  

438
00:16:04,584 --> 00:16:06,219
 to take charge                 
   of law enforcement.          

439
00:16:06,286 --> 00:16:07,988
- Agreed.                       

440
00:16:08,054 --> 00:16:10,924
  Do you have someone in mind?  

441
00:16:10,991 --> 00:16:12,792
   - I might.                   

442
00:16:12,859 --> 00:16:15,862
  - OK, I've cleaned the tablet 
   and ran a series of tests.   

443
00:16:15,929 --> 00:16:18,264
 - And?                         
 - Don't you get it, Mr. Trust? 

444
00:16:18,331 --> 00:16:19,699
  I mean, the--                 

445
00:16:19,766 --> 00:16:22,836
dude, I made the biggest        
   discovery in human history!  

446
00:16:22,902 --> 00:16:24,871
Oh, I can't wait                
to tell everyone.               

447
00:16:24,938 --> 00:16:26,706
  - All right.                  
 Slow down, kid.                

448
00:16:26,773 --> 00:16:28,475
A lot of people won't be        
 as excited about this,         

449
00:16:28,541 --> 00:16:31,044
   and some will find           
   it downright scary.          

450
00:16:31,111 --> 00:16:33,680
It'd be wise not to tell        
   anyone until we know more.   

451
00:16:33,747 --> 00:16:34,748
  - OK.                         

452
00:16:34,814 --> 00:16:36,282
 Well, I ran the                
  text from the tablet          

453
00:16:36,349 --> 00:16:39,652
 through every database we have 
  from both GSA and EF,         

454
00:16:39,719 --> 00:16:42,022
  and I ran it through          
an AI translator.               

455
00:16:42,088 --> 00:16:43,490
 No luck deciphering it.        

456
00:16:43,556 --> 00:16:45,492
  - And I'll be                 
  testing the material.         

457
00:16:45,959 --> 00:16:47,260
  - Whoa! Hey!                  
   What are you doing?          

458
00:16:47,327 --> 00:16:48,895
- It's a machined alloy,        
and it's stronger               

459
00:16:48,962 --> 00:16:51,765
than any material               
   I've ever heard of.          

460
00:16:51,831 --> 00:16:53,733
   - Machined?                  

461
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:55,135
 - Yep.                         

462
00:16:55,201 --> 00:16:57,937
   Which must mean that it was  
  made by an advanced society.  

463
00:16:58,004 --> 00:17:00,373
And the composite has an        
 element which I can't identify,

464
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:02,175
so it's probably                
 not from Earth.                

465
00:17:02,242 --> 00:17:04,477
 - Duh.                         

466
00:17:04,544 --> 00:17:07,781
 Dude, it's got                 
  alien writing on it.          

467
00:17:07,847 --> 00:17:10,583
   - We don't know it's alien.  

468
00:17:10,650 --> 00:17:13,920
- What else could it be?        

469
00:17:13,987 --> 00:17:16,790
  - Gentlemen,                  
  meet Milan Petrovic.          

470
00:17:16,856 --> 00:17:19,426
 Felix, he held                 
   the same position you did,   

471
00:17:19,492 --> 00:17:21,127
 but on my Ark.                 

472
00:17:21,194 --> 00:17:22,929
  - Civilian head of security?  

473
00:17:22,996 --> 00:17:26,766
 - And I can vouch for the fact 
  that he was quite good at it. 

474
00:17:26,833 --> 00:17:30,403
- I see you were a police       
 officer on Earth for 12 years. 

475
00:17:30,470 --> 00:17:33,606
- Tough time to be a cop,       
 when things were falling apart.

476
00:17:33,673 --> 00:17:36,609
   - Yes, yes.                  
It was, um, rough times.        

477
00:17:36,676 --> 00:17:38,912
 Everyone was panicking.        

478
00:17:38,978 --> 00:17:42,882
 - I'd like to take a day to ask
  around, do a little vetting.  

479
00:17:42,949 --> 00:17:45,151
 - Wasn't he vetted by the GSA? 

480
00:17:45,218 --> 00:17:47,320
 If I'm willing to accept that, 
  surely you would be.          

481
00:17:47,387 --> 00:17:48,788
   - Are you?                   

482
00:17:52,892 --> 00:17:55,261
- I'm willing to                
 accept him in the role.        

483
00:17:55,328 --> 00:17:57,630
   As long as some of           
his subordinates                

484
00:17:57,697 --> 00:17:59,599
 are former EF security.        

485
00:17:59,666 --> 00:18:01,868
   - That seems fair.           

486
00:18:01,935 --> 00:18:04,504
   - Then, you have a new job,  
  Mr. Petrovic.                 

487
00:18:04,571 --> 00:18:06,773
 Head of Homebase One Security. 

488
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:08,842
 - It'll be my pleasure.        

489
00:18:08,908 --> 00:18:12,378
   - As the colony is           
a frontier town of sorts,       

490
00:18:12,445 --> 00:18:15,682
might I suggest his title       
   be Sheriff?                  

491
00:18:15,748 --> 00:18:17,884
  - [chuckles]                  
  - [chuckles]                  

492
00:18:17,951 --> 00:18:21,988
 Sheriff Petrovic, you can start
  by taking over responsibility 

493
00:18:22,055 --> 00:18:25,625
   for the two people           
   we're holding in the jail.   

494
00:18:25,692 --> 00:18:28,394
   - Consider it done,          
 Mr. Strickland.                

495
00:18:28,461 --> 00:18:31,397
 [peaceful music]               

496
00:18:31,464 --> 00:18:33,133
   ♪ ♪                          

497
00:18:33,199 --> 00:18:36,102
 [birds singing]                

498
00:18:40,440 --> 00:18:43,343
 [sighs] Hello, ladies.         

499
00:18:43,409 --> 00:18:44,644
  I'm Sheriff Petrovic.         

500
00:18:44,711 --> 00:18:47,380
- Great.                        
  Keeps getting better.         

501
00:18:47,447 --> 00:18:48,781
  - Hi.                         

502
00:18:48,848 --> 00:18:50,783
 - I've been told I have        
  to check on you two.          

503
00:18:50,850 --> 00:18:53,186
Step back from the door.        

504
00:19:02,762 --> 00:19:04,931
  - Like what you see?          

505
00:19:04,998 --> 00:19:06,466
   - [scoffs]                   

506
00:19:07,467 --> 00:19:10,670
   I just wanted to make sure   
you've been treated well.       

507
00:19:10,737 --> 00:19:13,673
- Oh, I'm treated               
   like royalty here.           

508
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:15,175
  Look around.                  

509
00:19:16,676 --> 00:19:18,845
 [dramatic music]               

510
00:19:18,912 --> 00:19:20,013
[scoffs]                        

511
00:19:20,079 --> 00:19:22,081
  You're not very good          
at your new job, Sheriff.       

512
00:19:22,148 --> 00:19:25,852
- Sheriff, everything OK?       

513
00:19:25,919 --> 00:19:27,320
  Sir?                          

514
00:19:27,387 --> 00:19:28,688
[shouts]                        

515
00:19:28,755 --> 00:19:30,190
   ♪ ♪                          

516
00:19:30,256 --> 00:19:31,558
- Mm-mm.                        

517
00:19:31,624 --> 00:19:34,060
   - [groans]                   

518
00:19:34,127 --> 00:19:41,067
   ♪ ♪                          

519
00:19:52,212 --> 00:19:54,881
   [metallic creaking]          

520
00:20:02,789 --> 00:20:02,822
   [s                           

521
00:20:02,822 --> 00:20:02,855
   [sus                         

522
00:20:02,855 --> 00:20:02,889
   [suspe                       

523
00:20:02,889 --> 00:20:02,922
   [suspens                     

524
00:20:02,922 --> 00:20:02,956
   [suspensef                   

525
00:20:02,956 --> 00:20:02,989
   [suspenseful                 

526
00:20:02,989 --> 00:20:03,022
   [suspenseful m               

527
00:20:03,022 --> 00:20:03,056
   [suspenseful mus             

528
00:20:03,056 --> 00:20:03,089
   [suspenseful music           

529
00:20:03,089 --> 00:20:04,924
   [suspenseful music]          

530
00:20:04,991 --> 00:20:06,793
   - [groans]                   
   - Come here, mate.           

531
00:20:06,859 --> 00:20:08,394
  - I can walk.                 

532
00:20:08,461 --> 00:20:09,996
   - Your leg                   
  doesn't look so good.         

533
00:20:10,063 --> 00:20:12,498
  - I'll live.                  

534
00:20:12,565 --> 00:20:14,734
 - Sir, this is                 
  Lieutenant Kimi Joma.         

535
00:20:14,801 --> 00:20:17,303
- Lieutenant James Brice.       

536
00:20:17,370 --> 00:20:19,038
  - Medic Olga Morozov.         

537
00:20:19,105 --> 00:20:20,740
- And I'm Sharon Garnet,        
   captain of "Ark 1."          

538
00:20:20,807 --> 00:20:21,975
 - Admiral Ben Hammond,         

539
00:20:22,041 --> 00:20:23,910
   commanding officer           
   of "Ark 4."                  

540
00:20:23,977 --> 00:20:25,578
   - Admiral.                   

541
00:20:25,645 --> 00:20:28,114
   - Forgive me, sir.           
   I didn't realize your rank.  

542
00:20:28,181 --> 00:20:30,016
 - What happened                
   to Captain Lester?           

543
00:20:30,083 --> 00:20:33,286
- Uh, she's dead.               

544
00:20:33,353 --> 00:20:35,021
- Well, Lester would have       
  listened to me and not come.  

545
00:20:35,088 --> 00:20:36,889
  - Yeah, well,                 
 aren't you glad we did?        

546
00:20:36,956 --> 00:20:38,224
   Just a little bit?           

547
00:20:38,291 --> 00:20:39,592
 - Are there any                
other survivors?                

548
00:20:39,659 --> 00:20:41,294
  - No.                         

549
00:20:41,361 --> 00:20:42,862
  We only made it down          
  with two aft sections         

550
00:20:42,929 --> 00:20:44,330
  before the solar storms hit,  

551
00:20:44,397 --> 00:20:46,332
  so there weren't many         
  of us to start with.          

552
00:20:46,399 --> 00:20:47,567
And I fought next to them       

553
00:20:47,634 --> 00:20:49,636
  until every last one of them  
was dead.                       

554
00:20:49,702 --> 00:20:50,937
 - Fought who, exactly?         

555
00:20:51,004 --> 00:20:52,538
 [distant thud]                 

556
00:20:52,605 --> 00:20:55,875
 [distant growl]                

557
00:20:55,942 --> 00:20:57,744
  - They've done so much damage 
   to the structural integrity  

558
00:20:57,810 --> 00:20:59,946
  of this place, I'm surprised  
  it hasn't collapsed.          

559
00:21:00,013 --> 00:21:02,081
 - Again, who is "they"?        

560
00:21:02,148 --> 00:21:05,084
  [tense music]                 

561
00:21:05,151 --> 00:21:12,125
   ♪ ♪                          

562
00:21:12,191 --> 00:21:13,893
   - We have to hide.           

563
00:21:13,960 --> 00:21:16,029
  They come out at night time.  

564
00:21:16,095 --> 00:21:17,497
   - Who does?                  

565
00:21:17,563 --> 00:21:20,233
   [snarling]                   

566
00:21:20,300 --> 00:21:23,202
  [tense music]                 

567
00:21:23,269 --> 00:21:28,174
   ♪ ♪                          

568
00:21:28,241 --> 00:21:29,976
 - Run!                         
   Go! Go! Go! Go! Go!          

569
00:21:30,043 --> 00:21:32,478
   ♪ ♪                          

570
00:21:42,055 --> 00:21:43,723
Come on! Come on!               

571
00:21:47,093 --> 00:21:50,063
  [tense music]                 

572
00:21:50,129 --> 00:21:52,899
   [growling]                   

573
00:21:52,965 --> 00:21:59,806
   ♪ ♪                          

574
00:22:03,476 --> 00:22:05,378
   - What are those creatures?  

575
00:22:05,445 --> 00:22:07,213
  - Indigenous animals          
that we didn't know about       

576
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:09,349
 until after we got here        
   when they attacked.          

577
00:22:09,415 --> 00:22:10,683
   The only reason why          
we held on at all               

578
00:22:10,750 --> 00:22:12,118
 was because my science officer 
   figured out                  

579
00:22:12,185 --> 00:22:13,619
that they avoid sunlight        
   like fire.                   

580
00:22:13,686 --> 00:22:15,688
- So why didn't you flee        
during sunlight?                

581
00:22:15,755 --> 00:22:17,857
   - The solar storms           
  trashed our shuttles.         

582
00:22:17,924 --> 00:22:20,860
 Now those creatures are        
  trashing any shelter we have. 

583
00:22:20,927 --> 00:22:22,762
  - Well, good news is          
   we have an intact shuttle.   

584
00:22:22,829 --> 00:22:25,765
 - [sighs] I can't wait to tell 
 Eva about those things.        

585
00:22:25,832 --> 00:22:27,066
- Really?                       

586
00:22:27,133 --> 00:22:29,102
Telling Eva is the first        
  thing on your mind right now? 

587
00:22:29,168 --> 00:22:30,670
Wow, man, you got it bad.       

588
00:22:30,737 --> 00:22:32,138
 - What the hell                
are you on about?               

589
00:22:32,205 --> 00:22:33,539
  This is a huge deal.          

590
00:22:33,606 --> 00:22:36,809
  We're the first human beings  
 to ever see alien life.        

591
00:22:36,876 --> 00:22:38,044
   Except the "Ark 4" people.   

592
00:22:38,111 --> 00:22:39,345
 Sorry.                         

593
00:22:39,412 --> 00:22:40,747
- Well, you can tell Eva        
on your next nookie call,       

594
00:22:40,813 --> 00:22:43,349
  but first, we got to          
 get out of this alive.         

595
00:22:43,416 --> 00:22:44,517
   - You know about the calls?  

596
00:22:44,584 --> 00:22:46,386
- I'm in the next cabin,        
remember?                       

597
00:22:46,452 --> 00:22:48,287
  - The walls are pretty thin.  

598
00:22:48,354 --> 00:22:49,789
   ♪ ♪                          

599
00:22:49,856 --> 00:22:52,492
   [thud and rumbling]          

600
00:22:52,558 --> 00:22:54,761
 - I doubt this building's going
   to hold out until sunrise.   

601
00:22:54,827 --> 00:22:58,331
   ♪ ♪                          

602
00:22:58,398 --> 00:23:00,266
   - Garnet to Alicia and Ian.  

603
00:23:00,333 --> 00:23:01,801
 - We're here, Captain.         

604
00:23:01,868 --> 00:23:04,404
   - H-how's it going           
   down there, Sharon?          

605
00:23:04,470 --> 00:23:05,905
 - Not so good.                 
We're pinned down               

606
00:23:05,972 --> 00:23:08,007
  and won't be able to make it  
  to the shuttle until sunrise. 

607
00:23:08,074 --> 00:23:10,209
 - Wait.                        
  Pinned down by what?          

608
00:23:10,276 --> 00:23:12,011
 - Alien bloody creatures, mate.

609
00:23:12,078 --> 00:23:13,546
   Can you believe it?          

610
00:23:13,613 --> 00:23:16,582
   - Oh, really? Cool.          
 What do they look like?        

611
00:23:16,649 --> 00:23:18,684
   - All we know is they have   
an aversion to sunlight,        

612
00:23:18,751 --> 00:23:20,119
   so we have to wait           
 until the sun comes out        

613
00:23:20,186 --> 00:23:21,687
